on/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/369dde2e95b52356b71972b8f1103430e0a840d3d0478626dc5a78cba0f51a41.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $ABCD$ is shown above. Which of the following is ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′},$ the image of $ABCD$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-8093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 8 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-1,3),(0,6),(0,8),(-1,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-8093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "05374865d4d6a8be836ddaec0b406b0587de6ded509e6ed871d1bca1b6c8fa4f"} {"question" "q-8093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-8093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/0f5bf56cb4e4546079d89fd97b9533676116d4cff5435cbc926b925af5d3f637.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4672e5a5e616c8c6f4d40d1f29a2a8794da4e8d61c8bab09b7483ec292a91410.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/54496f08a7d4d6b5d37713ffe6d4bc903d48da90def0b273c114fb3b27e32bc9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/cb590309256b4836583132f707ceb405472d104908f5df45a0d41dfa18c84a30.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-8093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $XYZ$ has vertices at $X(2,0),\\,Y(-4,2),$ and $Z(4,0).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding vertices of  $△{X}^{′}{Y}^{′}{Z}^{′}$, the image of $△XYZ$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${X}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$),\\,$  $\\,{Y}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}}\n$),\\,$ $\\,{Z}^{′}($ \n{{field-5}}, \n{{field-6}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $△XYZ$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $XYZ:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{X}^{′}{Y}^{′}{Z}^{′}$ are ${X}^{′}(2,4),$\n${Y}^{′}(-12,-2),$ ${Z}^{′}(4,8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Develop