90291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "q-281563" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/state.json" "file_sha256" "189f499fd99732bc8822a37ca68683a0b9726dc38f8e1d007e1a266a07e5db54"} {"question" "e-6682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{AB}$ is shown below. Find $\\overline{A'B'},$ the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/2e22400d15abc81150d6d18de591549e81a46bd16ab9e95f10f86bae2125b490.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/example-6682.json" "file_sha256" "577cacda82d277c462d6ca9e496f23300a9854fecd91cc2217fb95198c6402fd"} {"question" "e-6682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are $A'(1,3)$ and $B'(1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/472f8e712f7d695d5ab3593dafbcc0309feb1a6ac80e9e29c4893dc651d20cf7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/example-6682.json" "file_sha256" "577cacda82d277c462d6ca9e496f23300a9854fecd91cc2217fb95198c6402fd"} {"question" "e-5447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below. Find $\\triangle A'B'C',$ the image of $\\triangle ABC$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4986bd2dab6b455c0d5e41909377c433d761bf7270364d3e2596e6db7f23981b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/example-5447.json" "file_sha256" "9dac8791ae857a52385be17ac4edcb03de05e1d473e475f4a54c5fc256c549a9"} {"question" "e-5447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\triangle ABC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $\\triangle A'B'C'$ are $(-3,-4), (0,0), (1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/e8a14ffbaab4597a2a4a7c9a6bad61dabbc4970fc22c3ed2eb28b2594a670938.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/example-5447.json" "file_sha256" "9dac8791ae857a52385be17ac4edcb03de05e1d473e475f4a54c5fc256c549a9"}] "usage" {"e-5447" [] "q-12031" [] "e-6682" []} "basis" 1012 "retractions" [[17592186087935 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/866/Source/Images/q-12031.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown above. Which of the following is $△A^{′}B^{′}C^{′}$, the image of $△ABC$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186087547 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{AB}$ is shown below. Find $\\overline{A'B'}$, the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/866/Source/Images/99de657ac0df287926da7644ba8c7907.png)"] [17592186087547 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are $A'(1,3)$ and $B'(1,-2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/866/Source/Images/105a1915ca20d2babc4684b44cd6620a.png)"] [17592186087501 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below. Find $\\triangle A'B'C'$, the image of $\\triangle ABC$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/866/Source/Images/8d714d1a8dad0fec63191b436f75f1f2.png)"] [17592186087501 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\triangle ABC$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $\\triangle A'B'C'$ are $(-3,-4), (0,0), (1,-2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/866/Source/Images/3ac3afc1cb31f4c0e1cf319acf62a417.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084622 17592186084623 17592186084624 17592186084625 17592186084626] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084621 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084621 :answer-field/id] [17592186084621 :answer-field/key] [17592186084621 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
